id: pm-rust-r9r2
title: Release commit identity was not allowlisted and the dangling-object test proved nothing
description: "Two defects in the new release gate, each of the kind where a check appears to work but does not. The release workflow configures the GitHub Actions bot as the commit author, but that address was missing from the approved identity list, so the first release would have written a commit that this very audit rejects on every subsequent run, permanently red. Separately the unreachable-object test amended a commit without resetting the author, so the bad identity remained on the reachable tip and the audit caught it from there. The test therefore passed identically whether or not the dangling-object walk worked at all."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[2]: gate-integrity,review
created_at: "2026-08-08T12:33:49.346Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-16T02:07:24.956Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-16T01:23:45.213Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-16T01:23:45.213Z"
author: pi-agent
acceptance_criteria: "The GitHub Actions bot identity used by the release commit is present in the approved list with a comment explaining why removing it would break the gate, and the unreachable-object test resets the author on amend so the bad identity exists only in the dangling commit, which is confirmed by the test continuing to pass while a mutation that disables the all-objects walk makes it fail."
comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-16T01:18:44.626Z",pi-agent,"Regression slice on branch release-pipeline-regression-contract-tests. New test dangling_identity_test_cannot_pass_vacuously in tests/identity_audit.rs builds the dangling scenario via a shared builder and asserts BOTH: (a) the bad identity (bad@unreachable.example) appears in NO reachable author (git log --all --format=%ae must be non-empty and clean — guarding against the empty-repo vacuity), and (b) the audit still rejects the repository, which with a clean ref walk can only happen via the unreachable commit. Release-workflow half: release_commit_identity_is_allowlisted_and_justified extracts every 'git config user.email' from the release job and requires each to be allowlisted, plus a justification comment referencing release.yml. Revert-proofs (both historical defects reproduced): mutating the audit to enumerate commits with 'git rev-list --all' instead of cat-file --batch-all-objects made the vacuity test FAIL (plus both dangling-object tests); removing --reset-author from the amend — the original vacuous form — made it FAIL with 'the bad identity is reachable from refs, so the dangling-object test proves nothing'; deleting the 41898282+github-actions[bot] identity from approved-git-identities.txt made the allowlist test FAIL. All restored, all green."
  "2026-08-16T02:07:24.956Z",claude-code,"CodeRabbit review round: the reachable-identity guard walked only %ae, so a bad address reachable solely as a committer would have made run_audit fail on a reachable commit while the dangling-object assertion still passed. The walk is now %ae%n%ce, extracted into reachable_identities so the guard and its regression test share one code path, and a new test builds a commit whose committer is unapproved and whose author is not. Reverting the walk to %ae fails that test. The git config user.email parser also accepted one exact spelling; --global, --local, --system and git -c user.email= are now all matched, proved by mutating release.yml to the --global form."
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-08T12:33:49.346Z",pi-agent,Both raised by CodeRabbit on pull request 6
files[2]{path,scope}:
  .github/approved-git-identities.txt,project
  tests/identity_audit.rs,project
tests[1]{command,scope}:
  cargo test --test identity_audit dangling_identity_test_cannot_pass_vacuously,project
close_reason: "Allowlist contract test in tests/release_pipeline_contract.rs and non-vacuous dangling-object guard dangling_identity_test_cannot_pass_vacuously in tests/identity_audit.rs, both revert-proofs (rev-list mutation and --reset-author removal) verified."
body: ""
